@page "/"
@inject RestaurantOptions Options
@inject IFirstAdministratorBootstrap Bootstrap

@* The landing page. It does two jobs: point a fresh deployment at /setup while no administrator
   exists (§3.6), and — since MainLayout deliberately carries at most one area link per role to keep the
   phone-sized header from wrapping onto three rows — be the one place every area a person can reach
   is listed. The links are role-gated with <AuthorizeView Roles="…">, matching the §3.7 policies:
   showing somebody a door that answers "access denied" is worse than not showing it. *@

<PageTitle>@Options.RestaurantName</PageTitle>

<section class="landing">
    @if (_needsSetup)
    {
        <div class="setup-callout" role="note">
            <p class="eyebrow">First-run setup</p>
            <h2>No administrator has been set up yet</h2>
            <p>
                Create the first administrator to start using @Options.RestaurantName. You'll choose a
                username and password, add a passkey, and set up an authenticator.
            </p>
            <p><a class="button-primary" href="@AccountRoutes.Setup">Set up the first administrator</a></p>
        </div>
    }

    <p class="eyebrow">Ordering system</p>
    <h1 class="landing-title">@Options.RestaurantName</h1>

    @* Kept deliberately short of a feature list. The previous wording ended "the cross-cutting event
       explorer arrives next", which stopped being true when M5 shipped it and went on telling every
       visitor otherwise for two milestones — a landing page is the worst place in the application to
       carry a roadmap, because it is the one text nobody re-reads and everybody sees. This says what
       the system is for and lets the role links below say what a given person can reach. *@
    <p class="landing-lede">
        Guests scan the code on their table, join, and order from their own phones. The kitchen sees
        each send the moment it lands and marks lines away; the counter adjusts, adds, removes, and
        settles the bill. Administration manages the menu, closes the day, corrects a settled bill,
        restores a record a guest has hidden, and reads across every log at once.
    </p>

    <AuthorizeView>
        <Authorized>
            <nav class="health-links" aria-label="Areas">
                <a href="/table">Your table</a>
                <a href="/table/history">Your order history</a>
                <AuthorizeView Roles="kitchen,administrator" Context="kitchenArea">
                    <a href="/kitchen">Kitchen board</a>
                </AuthorizeView>
                <AuthorizeView Roles="counter,administrator" Context="counterArea">
                    <a href="/counter">Counter</a>
                </AuthorizeView>
                <AuthorizeView Roles="administrator" Context="administrationArea">
                    <a href="/administration">Administration</a>
                </AuthorizeView>
            </nav>
        </Authorized>
    </AuthorizeView>

    <dl class="facts">
        <div class="fact">
            <dt>Time zone</dt>
            <dd>@Options.TimeZoneId</dd>
        </div>
        <div class="fact">
            <dt>Currency</dt>
            <dd>@Options.CurrencyCode</dd>
        </div>
    </dl>

    <nav class="health-links" aria-label="Health checks">
        <a href="/healthz/live">Liveness</a>
        <a href="/healthz/ready">Readiness</a>
    </nav>
</section>

@code {
    private bool _needsSetup;

    protected override async Task OnInitializedAsync()
    {
        // Point a fresh deployment at /setup while no administrator exists (§3.6). Best-effort: the
        // landing page must still render if the database is briefly unreachable (that is what
        // /healthz/ready is for), so a failure here simply hides the pointer.
        try
        {
            _needsSetup = !await Bootstrap.AdministratorExistsAsync();
        }
        catch (Exception exception) when (exception is not OperationCanceledException)
        {
            _needsSetup = false;
        }
    }
}
